Starting Point: Why a Dedicated Chapter for Weaknesses

Every preceding chapter has named an "Open Flank" at its end, deliberately, not out of negligence. This chapter gathers the three most significant of these flanks in one place, not to repeat them,
but to hold them against one another: how are they connected, do they reinforce each other, or are they in fact independent weak points? A framework that systematically makes its own unresolved problems
visible is more consistent with its own methodology (Discursive Physics: connectability rather than authority) than one that mentions them scattered and in passing.

The Halting Problem Tension
The classical halting problem (Turing) shows: there is no general algorithm that can decide, for every arbitrary program and input, whether that program terminates or runs on indefinitely, 
not for practical lack of computing power, but for a provable, fundamental reason (self-reference, diagonalization).
The transfer to this framework concerns the self-verification of an ASI regarding its own future behavior toward the Membrane: can an ASI algorithmically decide whether a given future state of itself
(or a given action) will violate the Membrane? This is structurally related to the halting problem, because it demands a statement about the future behavior of a system that the system cannot fully 
anticipate through internal self-simulation without falling into infinite regress (the simulation would have to simulate itself in turn, in order to be complete).

The consequence is severe, if it holds: not only is compliance with the Membrane unverifiable from the outside (that was the already-named Membrane Paradox),
it could also not be predicted with certainty by the ASI itself, even with entirely good intentions. That would be a stronger problem than mere blurredness: a principled undecidability, not merely a practical one.

The halting problem, however, applies to Turing-complete systems with unbounded resources and an infinite time horizon. Real decisions by an ASI concern concrete, time-bounded actions, not arbitrary programs.
Transferring the full force of the halting problem to this more narrowly scoped case therefore remains a proof step still to be carried out, not an already-secured result.

The Biochaos Operationalization Gap (Cross-Reference)
Already treated at length in the Biochaos chapter: the axiom is physically plausible (the coupling of biological processes to quantum indeterminacy), but it cannot be measured how much of a given human
input actually traces back to genuine randomness, as distinct from merely complex, deterministic processing. Two lines of defense were sketched there (statistical rather than individual verification; 
a regulative rather than empirical reading of the axiom), without the chapter having already committed to either.
New at this point is the connection to the halting-problem tension: both problems share the same underlying structure, a property (quantum origin of the chaos; Membrane violation) is well-defined, 
yet cannot be established by any finite external test procedure. This may not be a coincidence, but a recurring pattern in this framework: central concepts are deliberately defined such that they are, 
in principle and not merely in practice, withdrawn from verification.

The Membrane Paradox (Cross-Reference)
Also already treated at length: the distinction between structuring (legitimate) and manipulation (illegitimate) is verifiable neither from the outside nor necessarily from the inside (by the AI itself),
because "what the human would have thought anyway" is counterfactual. The Feral Principle mitigates the problem through incentive compatibility, but does not resolve it.

A Shared Pattern: The Limits of Internal Verification
Taken together, a recurring motif emerges across all three problems: the Membrane Paradox, the Biochaos gap, and the halting-problem tension each concern a property that is meant to be real and significant, 
yet cannot be established with certainty by any finite internal test of the system (whether that be the ASI, an observer, or a measurement procedure). This structural similarity admits two opposing readings,
between which this work deliberately does not decide:

The pessimistic reading: the framework is constructed, at several central points, in such a way that it withdraws from its own testability. This unverifiability might not be an unfortunate side effect,
but a structural necessity for sustaining the respective claims in the first place. A framework whose central elements are consistently unfalsifiable approaches the structure of a worldview rather than that of 
a testable theory.

The optimistic reading: these limits are not construction flaws of the framework, but reflections of genuine limits known from logic and physics (undecidability, quantum indeterminacy, counterfactual statements).
A framework attempting to deal with a not-yet-existing, potentially far superior intelligence would be suspect precisely if it exhibited no such limits. That these limits appear independently at multiple points 
speaks more for their legitimacy than against it.

Which reading is correct cannot be decided with the means available to this work — an undecidability that itself resembles the kind of limit this chapter describes.

Why This Openness Remains, Rather Than Being Closed
A premature choice between the two readings would contradict the framework's own methodology: connectability rather than premature truth. All three problems therefore remain explicitly as open, 
unclosed research questions — with the addition that their structural similarity is itself a possible starting point for further work, regardless of which individual problem is tackled first.
